From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 23:55:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] net: ipa: run the v4.11 core clock at the nominal tier

Downstream scales the IPA core clock across four tiers and picks one from
measured throughput:

	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_SVS2		 60 MHz
	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_SVS		125 MHz
	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_NOMINAL	220 MHz
	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_TURBO		250 MHz

Mainline has one number and it is 60 MHz, the bottom tier, held forever.

That is enough for GPRS, which is what works here. The open question is an
LTE attach, where the modem programs its channels and then hangs hard enough
that it never records a reason, and its Q6 watchdog takes the chip down about
ten seconds later. A GSI command that does not complete would look exactly
like that.

This is a test of that idea, not a considered fix. The right shape is what
downstream does -- scale with demand -- and pinning the nominal tier costs
power for no benefit on a link that never needs it.
---
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/data/ipa_data-v4.11.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/data/ipa_data-v4.11.c
@@ -387,7 +387,18 @@
 
 /* Clock and interconnect configuration data for an SoC having IPA v4.11 */
 static const struct ipa_power_data ipa_power_data = {
-	.core_clock_rate	= 60 * 1000 * 1000,	/* Hz */
+	/* Downstream scales this with throughput across four tiers, and only
+	 * the lowest of them is 60 MHz:
+	 *
+	 *	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_SVS2		 60 MHz
+	 *	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_SVS		125 MHz
+	 *	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_NOMINAL	220 MHz
+	 *	IPA_V4_0_CLK_RATE_TURBO		250 MHz
+	 *
+	 * Mainline pins the lowest one and never moves. GPRS never notices.
+	 * An LTE attach might, so run at the nominal tier and see.
+	 */
+	.core_clock_rate	= 220 * 1000 * 1000,	/* Hz */
 	.interconnect_count	= ARRAY_SIZE(ipa_interconnect_data),
 	.interconnect_data	= ipa_interconnect_data,
 };
